title: Context menus
tags: GUI
kind: reference
aliases: context-menu right-click menu file-actions
summary: What a right-click offers — the metarecord's operations, the file actions, the text, the view — always in the same order, and how to drive a menu from the keyboard.

The web view's own menu (back, reload, inspect…) never appears, except over an
editable text field, which keeps it for cut, copy and paste. Everywhere else a
right-click opens the GUI's own menu.

!! What it offers

Its entries come under headers, always in this order, so the same right-click reads
the same way in every panel:

|!Header |!Entries |
|''Metarecord'' |the panel's operations on the clicked metarecord: open it in `metarecord-detail` or `file`, show its folder in the file manager or in `metarecord-list` ([[Cross-panel selection]]), add it to or remove it from the checked selection, copy its uuid, and what the panel adds (delete it…) |
|''File'' |the ''file actions'': Open with… ([[Opening a file with another program]]), Cut, Copy, Paste, Rename…, Duplicate, Copy full path, Move to trash |
|''Directory'' |in the file manager: New folder…, New file… |
|''Ignore'' |ignore patterns for the clicked path ([[Setting ignore patterns]]) |
|''Text'' |''Copy'' — the text selected when the menu opened; greyed out with none |
|''View'' |''Split / unsplit'', ''Swap panel types'', ''Open web inspector'' (`panel:toggle split`, `panel:swap`, `devtools:open`) |

A panel's single primary action — ''Keep this copy'' in the [[duplicates panel]], a
picker's ''Pick this folder'' — comes first, above the headers. A header with nothing
under it is left out, and one rule separates two headers.

The file actions are the same in every panel showing files — the file manager, the
metarecord list and detail, the `file`, `recent` and `duplicates` panels, the
`mfr_path` tree of `treeref` — and share one clipboard: what is cut or copied in one
is pasted in another. ''Copy full path'' copies the file's absolute path as text.

Right-clicking where no panel offers anything — the tab bar's empty space, a slot
header, the status bar, a panel's background — leaves only ''Text'' and ''View''. A
right-click on a tab does not open a menu: it shows that workspace in the other slot
([[Workspaces]]).

!! From the keyboard

Up and Down move in the menu, Enter runs the entry, Escape or a click outside closes
it. Typing jumps to the first entry starting with what was typed; the typed text
resets after a second's pause, and repeating one letter cycles through the entries
starting with it. Headers are skipped. While a menu is open, the keys go to it and no
keybinding fires.

The menu is styled by the classes `mf-menu`, `mf-menu-item` (with `active` and
`disabled`), `mf-menu-header` and `mf-menu-separator`, which the user stylesheet can
override.

!! In a panel

A panel opens its own menu with `metafolder.contextMenu(event, items)`, or adds
entries to the default one with `metafolder.contextMenu.addDefaultItems(event =>
items)`. An item is `{label, action?, disabled?}`, `{header}`, or `-` for a separator.
Every menu is put in order before it is shown (`orderMenu` in `/__menu.js`): known
headers in the order above, unknown ones after them, two groups under the same header
merged, and the separators ''derived'' — a `-` passed by a panel is dropped and one
rule is drawn at each boundary between headers, so that a rule always means a new
section. A menu without any header (a two-entry row menu, the panel menu of a slot) is
shown as given, its separators included.

The test "is this a text field?" looks at the event's ''composed'' target
(`composedPath()[0]`): a right-click inside a panel reaches the shell's listener
retargeted to the panel's host element, and testing `event.target` would take the
native menu — its cut, copy and paste — away from every text field of every panel.
